{"subjectId":"python","id":"variables-types","title":"Variables, Types & Operators","group":"Getting Started","order":3,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A variable is a <em>name bound to an object</em>. Every object has an identity, a type and a value.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Name binding</strong> — <code>x = 10</code> creates an <code>int</code> object and binds the name <code>x</code> to it. Assignment never copies — it only attaches another name to the same object.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Built-in types</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example</th><th>Mutable?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>int</code></td><td><code>42</code>, <code>-7</code>, <code>10**100</code> (unlimited size)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>float</code></td><td><code>3.14</code>, <code>1e-9</code> (64-bit IEEE 754)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>complex</code></td><td><code>2 + 3j</code></td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>bool</code></td><td><code>True</code>, <code>False</code> (a subclass of <code>int</code>)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>str</code></td><td><code>'hi'</code>, <code>\"hi\"</code>, <code>'''multi'''</code></td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>NoneType</code></td><td><code>None</code> — the single “no value” object</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>list</code> / <code>dict</code> / <code>set</code></td><td><code>[1, 2]</code>, <code>{'a': 1}</code>, <code>{1, 2}</code></td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>tuple</code> / <code>frozenset</code></td><td><code>(1, 2)</code>, <code>frozenset({1})</code></td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>x = 10\nprint(type(x))                    # &lt;class 'int'&gt;\nx = \"now a string\"                # same name, different object and type\nprint(isinstance(x, str))         # True\nprint(True + True)                # 2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math-symbols\"></i>Operators at a glance</h3>\n<p>Arithmetic <code>+ - * / // % **</code>, comparison <code>== != &lt; &lt;= &gt; &gt;=</code> (chainable), logical <code>and or not</code>, identity <code>is</code>, membership <code>in</code>, bitwise <code>&amp; | ^ ~ &lt;&lt; &gt;&gt;</code> and the walrus <code>:=</code>. The next topic, <strong>Operators &amp; Precedence</strong>, covers each one in depth.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(7 / 2, 7 // 2, 7 % 2, -7 // 2)   # 3.5 3 1 -4\nprint(2 ** 10, divmod(17, 5))          # 1024 (3, 2)\nprint(0 &lt; 5 &lt; 10)                      # True\nprint(\"\" or \"default\", 0 and 99)       # default 0\nif (n := len(\"hello\")) &gt; 3:\n    print(n)                           # 5</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i><code>==</code> vs <code>is</code></h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i><code>==</code> — equality</h3><p>Calls <code>__eq__</code>: do the two objects have the <em>same value</em>?</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-fingerprint\"></i><code>is</code> — identity</h3><p>Are they the <em>same object</em> in memory (same <code>id</code>)? Use only for singletons: <code>None</code>, <code>True</code>, <code>False</code>.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>a = [1, 2, 3]\nb = [1, 2, 3]\nc = a\nprint(a == b, a is b, a is c)   # True False True</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>CPython caches small integers (−5…256) and some strings, so <code>a is b</code> may be <code>True</code> for them by accident. Never rely on it — compare values with <code>==</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-toggle-left\"></i>Truthiness</h3>\n<p>In a condition, every object is truthy except these <strong>falsy</strong> values:</p>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\"><code>False</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>None</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>0</code>, <code>0.0</code>, <code>0j</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>''</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>[]</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>()</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>{}</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>set()</code></span><span class=\"chip\"><code>range(0)</code></span></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>items = []\nif not items:              # idiomatic emptiness check\n    print(\"empty\")         # empty\nprint(bool(\"0\"), bool(0))  # True False</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-transform\"></i>Type conversion</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(int(\"42\") + 1, float(\"2.5\"), str(99) + \"!\")   # 43 2.5 99!\nprint(int(3.99), round(2.5), round(3.5))            # 3 2 4\nprint(int(\"ff\", 16), bin(10), hex(255))             # 255 0b1010 0xff</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>round()</code> uses <em>banker's rounding</em>: halves go to the nearest even number, so <code>round(2.5) == 2</code>.</p></div></section>"}